  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 119 PM CDT Fri Aug 28 2026\n\nCurrent water vapor imagery and RAP analysis highlight the mid/upper \nridge sliding eastward off the Four Corners as an upper shortwave \nbegins to enter the Pacific Northwest. At the surface, high pressure \nhas shifted toward the Mississippi River as lee troughing commences \nacross the Northern High Plains. Gusty south winds arising from the \nresultant tightening surface pressure gradient have been observed \nacross the forecast area today. Most of the area should remain dry \nthrough the overnight hours and beyond, though a handful of light \nshowers may find their way into central Kansas early Saturday \nmorning per the latest short-term guidance. These should be low-\nimpact if they manage to hold together, and silent PoPs were \nmaintained along/north of I-70 owing to low confidence in coverage \nand the general ability for activity to maintain itself this far east.\n\nBreezy south winds will continue through the weekend and into next \nweek ahead of the surface trough exiting the High Plains. As the \nupper ridge continues its slide eastward, hot temperatures will \nreturn to the forecast area with highs expected to reach into the \n100-105 degree range for most locations Saturday and Sunday. This \nheat is expected to continue for most of next week as we remain \nunder the influence of the mid/upper high, with highs near 100 \ndegrees forecast through at least Thursday. Thanks to widespread \nsubsidence, most of the area will remain dry through this period, \nthough portions of central Kansas may get in on some isolated \nshowers and storms during the late evening and overnight hours on \nMonday and Tuesday as the surface boundary slowly shifts to the \neast. Better chances look to reside to our northwest, however, so we \nare not expecting much in the way of meaningful rainfall through at \nleast the middle of next week and likely beyond.",
